\begin{equation}
\frac {(x + 1)!}{(x + 1-x)!} = \frac{(x+1)!}{(x+1 - x)!} \div (x + 1 - x)! = (x + 1)!
\end{equation}
Now if instead we had multipl ied by $(x + 1 - x)$! which is apparently not an equivalent form of $\frac {(x + 1 - x)!}{(x + 1 - x)!}  \times  (x + 1)!$, 
\begin{equation}
\frac{(x+1- x)\times (x + 1) }{(x + 1-x)!} \times\frac {(x + 1)!}{(x + 1-x)!} = ((x+1 - x) \times  (x + 1))!
\end{equation}
Now we see that this extra multiple of $(x + 1) $ faithfully incorporates the extra $(x + 1)$ in the original quotient and hence we see that this extra $\times (x + 1)$ is always $1$ incidentally!
